From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:33:12 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 156/167] hjviewer: App Store installs update in the App Store
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The iOS update screen took every App Store Connect export for a TestFlight
install, so an app from the App Store would have been sent to TestFlight,
which does not have it. TestFlight and the App Store hand out the same
build; what tells them apart is the app's receipt. package_info_plus 8.0.2
already reads it on iOS without Swift: installerStore is "com.apple" for the
App Store's receipt and "com.apple.testflight" for a sandbox receipt.

HjvHost reads it with the device at the start (deviceValue) and keeps it for
the screens only: the core judges every iPhone by the same policy entry, so
hjv-update-device stays as it was. HjvDevice.appStore is an App Store Connect
build whose receipt is the App Store's; it gets "App Store에서 HJViewer의
[업데이트]를 누르세요. ..." and [App Store 열기], which opens
https://apps.apple.com/app/id6821290058 outside the app (the toast says what
to do when it does not open). TestFlight installs keep their texts and
button, and so does any App Store Connect build whose receipt is not known
to be the App Store's; other exports still ask the administrator.

App Review runs the build with a sandbox receipt as well, so its copy shows
TestFlight's way if the policy is ahead of the version under review.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
